# Private "artifactory" alternative to public npm.
#
# package.json's `publishConfig` currently points at public npm
# (registry.npmjs.org, scope @jummon, access "public"). If Jummon decides
# to gate this SDK behind a private registry instead of publishing it in
# the open (e.g. while it's still pre-GA, or for enterprise-only builds),
# swap the target here — no source change required, only publishConfig +
# this .npmrc.
#
# 1. Provision a GCP Artifact Registry npm repository:
#      gcloud artifacts repositories create jummon-npm \
#        --repository-format=npm \
#        --location=us-central1 \
#        --description="Jummon private npm packages"
#
# 2. Point the @jummon scope at it (copy this file to .npmrc, uncomment):
#
# @jummon:registry=https://us-central1-npm.pkg.dev/<GCP_PROJECT_ID>/jummon-npm/
# //us-central1-npm.pkg.dev/<GCP_PROJECT_ID>/jummon-npm/:always-auth=true
#
# 3. Auth (CI): use google-artifactregistry-auth (Google's official helper)
#    rather than a long-lived token in .npmrc:
#      npx google-artifactregistry-auth
#    This mints a short-lived token from the runner's GCP credentials
#    (Workload Identity / service account) and writes it into .npmrc for
#    the duration of the job — never commit a token.
#
# 4. package.json publishConfig would then read:
#      "publishConfig": {
#        "access": "restricted",
#        "registry": "https://us-central1-npm.pkg.dev/<GCP_PROJECT_ID>/jummon-npm/"
#      }
#
# Consumers (customer apps, or Jummon's own repos) add the same
# "@jummon:registry=..." line to their own .npmrc (or CI config) to resolve
# @jummon/* from the private registry instead of public npm.
